1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:03,380
Well, let's see how we can finish off our first game.

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:08,240
Now, just like with the dragonfly, we need to sense if we are touching the sprite.

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:14,810
So I'm quite sure you know what to do here, because basically it's exactly the same as the dragonfly

4
00:00:14,810 --> 00:00:20,360
has, except instead of the dragonfly sensing, if it is touching the ladybug, it's the opposite way

5
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:20,960
around.

6
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:23,990
But I want to show you another little trick here.

7
00:00:24,630 --> 00:00:29,040
Remember how earlier we learned we could duplicate code in a sprite?

8
00:00:29,570 --> 00:00:32,750
But we can also duplicate code in between sprites.

9
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:38,870
So going back to our dragonfly, we can simply click and drag the script and drop it onto our ladybug

10
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:39,530
sprite.

11
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:42,740
And doing this will automatically duplicate the script.

12
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:48,140
Note that now too has this script, so that's cool.

13
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:53,720
Now all we need to do is change it from touching the ladybugs to touching the dragonflies.

14
00:00:53,810 --> 00:00:59,600
And if you haven't yet figured out how to delete code, you just drag it into this area here and we'll

15
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:00,200
delete.

16
00:01:00,990 --> 00:01:06,720
Now, what we want it to do is hide when it touches a dragonfly, almost like it's being eaten.

17
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:14,430
So here we can just go to the looks tab, bring in the hide code so our sprite hides when it touches

18
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:15,300
the dragonfly.

19
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:21,060
Now if we play again, notice if we touch the ladybug, it hides.

20
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:27,710
But unfortunately we have some problems because it's sort of kind of stays hidden and we don't want

21
00:01:27,710 --> 00:01:28,100
that.

22
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:32,180
And secondly, our dragonfly doesn't play the sound that we wanted.

23
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:37,270
But this brings us to an important aspect of programming debugging.

24
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:41,740
And no, I'm not talking about Our Lady bug, even though it's a sort of funny coincidence.

25
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:47,380
Debugging is essentially the process of finding and fixing errors in your code.

26
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:50,560
And in programming, there are two main types of errors.

27
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:53,740
Firstly, they are what are called syntax errors.

28
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:56,230
And basically all that means is these are errors.

29
00:01:56,230 --> 00:02:02,950
When you write code that doesn't follow the correct syntax or the rules of how the code and the programming

30
00:02:02,950 --> 00:02:04,180
language should be written.

31
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:08,650
Syntax errors usually prevent your code from running at all, but thankfully you don't have to worry

32
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:11,680
about this because scratch prevents these errors.

33
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:15,550
Because you might try to connect two blocks that don't really fit together.

34
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:17,560
And this is basically saying no.

35
00:02:17,590 --> 00:02:20,680
Doing this would cause essentially a type of syntax error.

36
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:28,810
But basically scratch is forcing you to write with the use of blocks, perfect syntax, error free code,

37
00:02:28,810 --> 00:02:30,730
which ain't a bad thing.

38
00:02:31,270 --> 00:02:38,050
However, the next type of bug are those that have to do with logical errors, and these errors occur

39
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:42,460
when your code is written correctly but doesn't do what you intended it to do.

40
00:02:42,490 --> 00:02:49,900
For example, in our game, if the ladybug doesn't hide when it should or if it hides too soon, that

41
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:51,550
would be a logical error.

42
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:58,000
Your code still runs, but the result is not what you expected, and these errors will occur all the

43
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:58,840
time in coding.

44
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:00,400
But that's just part of programming.

45
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:04,450
You should never expect your program to work perfectly on the first try.

46
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:06,940
You'll really be quite lucky if that does happen.

47
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:13,270
Instead, it's all about debugging and finding all the errors that are in your code and getting rid

48
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:14,950
of them to get it to work well.

49
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:19,270
And also, as you gain more experience, you'll get better at spotting and fixing these errors.

50
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:26,330
So with that all said, let's take a look at these logical errors and see if we can fix them.

51
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:33,350
So looking at the script for Our Lady Bug, you can see that we get it to hide when it touches the dragonfly,

52
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:37,040
but we never get it to show again.

53
00:03:37,370 --> 00:03:42,050
So what we could do is bring in a show block right below this.

54
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:48,150
But you should also notice right away that this is just going to hide and show right away, and happen

55
00:03:48,150 --> 00:03:50,340
so fast that it looks like nothing happens.

56
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:52,830
And we can confirm this by clicking play.

57
00:03:53,790 --> 00:03:55,740
You can see it doesn't really hide.

58
00:03:56,730 --> 00:03:57,630
What we need.

59
00:03:57,630 --> 00:04:03,120
Here is a weight code block so we can have the sprite hide and then show after a bit of time.

60
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:07,080
So adding it in between these two blocks and then playing it again.

61
00:04:07,470 --> 00:04:11,280
Yes, you can see it hides properly and then it shows again for a second.

62
00:04:11,910 --> 00:04:18,780
However, we still have one more bug and that is that our dragonfly is not saying yum and making that

63
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:19,530
cool sound.

64
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:23,820
Now this area is a bit harder as our code seems perfect.

65
00:04:24,670 --> 00:04:31,000
To fix this, we need to think like a computer and figure out why it's not working as expected.

66
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:32,980
Let's analyze the situation.

67
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:39,000
If we look at our dragonfly code when it touches the ladybug, it's supposed to say yum and play a sound,

68
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:39,690
right?

69
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:46,510
However, when we observe the game, the ladybug hides immediately upon touching the dragonfly, which

70
00:04:46,510 --> 00:04:52,780
means the dragonfly doesn't get a chance to detect that it's touching the ladybug.

71
00:04:53,200 --> 00:05:00,940
So to fix this issue, we can add a short delay before the ladybug hides, allowing the dragonfly time

72
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,850
to register that it's touching the ladybug.

73
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:11,320
And we can do this simply by adding a white block before the hide block in the ladybugs code, and setting

74
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:15,700
the wait time to something short like 0.1 seconds.

75
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:22,460
This will create a brief pause before the ladybug hides, but it gives the dragonfly enough time to

76
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:27,230
sense it's touching the ladybug, but it still appears almost instantaneous to us.

77
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:29,090
So let's test the code again.

78
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:30,980
And there you have it.

79
00:05:31,490 --> 00:05:36,500
Our very first game is working and all the bugs have been squished and debugged.

80
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:40,310
Really, really great work on getting to this point.

81
00:05:40,310 --> 00:05:47,510
You are now pretty much I could say a game developer, so put that on your resume, but get ready to

82
00:05:47,510 --> 00:05:52,340
make some far more exciting games than this in the next sections.

